            0.162
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.331,
            0.457,
            0.356
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.1 Doubling a Square"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.364,
            0.846,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": "In Baudhāyana’s Śulba-Sūtra (c. 800 BCE), Baudhāyana considers the following question:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.413,
            0.698,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "? How can one construct a square having double the area of a given square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.455,
            0.702,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "A first guess might be to simply double the length of each side of the square. Will this new square have double the area of the original square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.711,
            0.405,
            0.846,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.511,
            0.846,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "It’s not hard to see that the new square will have area $2 \\times 2 = 4$ times the area of the original square:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.245,
            0.569,
            0.401,
            0.719
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.558,
            0.753,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.767,
            0.845,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": "So how can one make a square that has double the area? Baudhāyana in his Śulba-Sūtra (Verse 1.9) gave an elegant answer to this question:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.825,
            0.828,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": "The diagonal of a square produces a square of double the area of the original square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.883,
            0.845,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": "As Baudhāyana says, the key is to construct a square on the diagonal of the original square:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
